it's harvest time in indian-administered  kashmir where apple production  contributes 10 percent  to the state's 20 billion income  but this year apples are being left to  rot because farmers can't find people to  pick them  after prime minister narendra modi  abolished the state's autonomous status  and imposed a security lockdown  the situation is bad we couldn't harvest  the apples on time and most of them fell  down from the trees  now we're collecting the rotten apples  and throwing them away  internet and mobile phone services  remain cut off  for this farmer that means his business  is in jeopardy  when the basic communication is down we  are not able to  talk to the people uh no who were  sitting in delhi who are staying in  bangalore who are staying in  chennai and other big markets when we  are not able to communicate with them  it's really difficult this wholesale  fruit market in srinagar would normally  be packed with traders  businesses down by 95 per cent this year  compared to last  sssp each year i export about 200  000 boxes of apples in the domestic  markets across india  this year because of the situation  farmers aren't able to send their  produce to market  they don't know where to send it because  communication lines aren't working  in response to the crisis the indian  government has set up a national  cooperative through which farmers can  sell their produce  but it imposes strict quality control  measures  which many farmers find hard to meet  we can only select the produce if the  color of the apple is good  so far we have rejected 80 percent of  the farmers who've come here to sell  their produce  the apple industry in kashmir involves  3.5 million people  around half the population of the state  the lockdown has devastated the economy  and farmers are calling on the indian  government to immediately lift the  security restrictions  victoria gatenby al jazeera
 

